<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
The companion app as a per-user LaunchAgent.

The other half of the startup inversion ADR 0002 describes. On a separated
install the daemon is a LaunchDaemon with no session at all, so what
autostarts in the user's login session is
the menu-bar companion (src/privacyfence/companion.py) -- Open Approvals,
Open Settings, Quit, and the OPEN <url> channel the daemon's connector OAuth
flows hand a browser URL to. Without it a separated install has no human-
facing affordance except asking the MCP client for a link, which is the
courier pattern privilege separation exists to retire.

Installed to /Library/LaunchAgents/com.privacyfence.companion.plist (root:wheel
0644) rather than ~/Library/LaunchAgents: the separated install is machine-
wide, so every user who logs in gets the companion, and the one who ran the
installer is additionally in the service group that lets it actually connect.
launchd starts one copy per login session, which is exactly right -- the
companion holds no state and its socket lives under the shared handoff
directory.

scripts/macos_privilege_separation.sh fills in every placeholder below.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>__COMPANION_LABEL__</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>__COMPANION_EXECUTABLE__</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>

  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>5</integer>

  <!-- A menu-bar item is a UI element, unlike the daemon above. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Interactive</string>
  <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key>
  <string>Aqua</string>
</dict>
</plist>
